---
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import CTASection from '../../../components/CTASection.astro';
import { RELATIONSHIP } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'The OEM Battery Development Process | Dingwei Battery';
const description = 'The step-by-step OEM battery development process — from requirement to shipment — and where buyers should be involved.';
const articleTitle = 'The OEM battery development process';
---
<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} articleTitle={articleTitle}>
  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge</span>
    <h1>The OEM battery development process</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-01 · Source: Chengguang Energy technical documentation</p>
    <p class="lead">OEM development is a gated process. Understanding the stages tells you where to be involved and where to insist on sign-off.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> OEM battery development moves through consultation, specification, design and branding, sampling, mass production, quality inspection, and shipping. Buyers should be most involved at specification, sampling, inspection and shipment, because those are the gates that prevent specification drift.
    </div>

    <h2>The seven stages</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Consultation</li>
      <li>Specification</li>
      <li>Design &amp; branding</li>
      <li>Sampling</li>
      <li>Mass production</li>
      <li>Quality inspection</li>
      <li>Logistics &amp; shipping</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Where buyers should be involved</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Specification — sign off every parameter</li>
      <li>Sampling — test before approving</li>
      <li>Quality inspection — agree the acceptance criteria</li>
      <li>Shipment — confirm documentation</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why gates matter</h2>
    <p>Each gate is a decision point. Skipping a gate is how a specification drifts and a repeat order comes back different.</p>
    <p><a href="/oem/">Explore OEM programs &rarr;</a> · <a href="/contact/">Talk through your development project &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>{RELATIONSHIP}</p>

    <h2>Where the buyer must sign off</h2>
    <p>Four gates need a buyer signature, not just a manufacturer confirmation:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Specification</strong> — sign every parameter, including the CCA test basis.</li>
      <li><strong>Sample</strong> — test the pre-production sample before approval.</li>
      <li><strong>Quality standard</strong> — agree acceptance criteria before mass production.</li>
      <li><strong>Shipment</strong> — confirm the documents before release.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Skip one gate and the next order can drift from the approved sample. Keep the sign-off sequence tied to the <a href="/knowledge/how-oem-battery-programs-work/">OEM program phases</a> so both sides know where approval is required.</p>
  </section>

  <CTASection title="Plan your OEM development process" />
</ArticleLayout>
